%I A399514 #13 Sep 23 2026 08:50:26
%S A399514 4,356,2068,36652,1024100,83300,2327500,796250,3613750,27641250,
%T A399514 125448750,959546250,4354863750,1402413750,2189733750,12720138750,
%U A399514 4817414250,27984305250,10598311350,61565471550,23316284970,135444037410,51295826934,297976882302
%N A399514 Trajectory of 4 under Conway's PIGAME.
%C A399514 See A395539 for the definition of the PIGAME successor function and many related references.
%C A399514 Since a(1) = 89*2^k with k = 2, the next power of two in the sequence is expected to be 2^pi(2), where pi(2) = 4 is the 2nd decimal of Pi. (Similarly, in A399511 with A399511(1) = 89*2^0, the next power of two is A399511(776) = 8 = 2^3, corresponding to the Oth decimal, i.e., the integer part of Pi.)
%C A399514 However, we do not know at which index the number 16 = 2^4, or any other power of two, will appear in this sequence.
%H A399514 M. F. Hasler, <a href="/A399514/b399514.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..100</a>
%F A399514 a(n+1) = A395539(a(n)) for all n >= 0.
%e A399514 a(0) = 4 is the initial value. To get the next term a(1), we must apply Conway's PIGAME function A395539 to this initial value, i.e., a(1) =  A395539(4) = 356.
%e A399514 (Here we could use a direct look up, but we could also search the first denominator that divides 4 in the list A350556: it's the last one, A350556(40) = 1, so we simply multiply with the corresponding numerator, A350555(40) = 89, to get a(2) = 4 * 89.)
%e A399514 To get a(2), we note that the first denominator that divides a(1) is A350556(15) = 89, so we divide by this and multiply with the numerator A350555(15) = 517 = 11 * 47. Hence, a(2) = 2^2 * 11 * 47 = 2068. The next values are:
%e A399514 a(3) = 2^2 * 7^2 * 11 * 17 = 36652.
%e A399514 a(4) = 2^2 * 5^2 * 7^2 * 11 * 19 = 1024100.
%e A399514 a(5) = 2^2 * 5^2 * 7^2 * 17 = 83300.
%e A399514 a(6) = 2^2 * 5^4 * 7^2 * 19 = 2327500.
%e A399514 a(7) = 2 * 5^4 * 7^2 * 13 = 796250.
%e A399514 a(8) = 2 * 5^4 * 7^2 * 59 = 3613750.
%e A399514 a(9) = 2 * 3^5 * 5^4 * 7 * 13 = 27641250.
%e A399514 a(10) = 2 * 3^5 * 5^4 * 7 * 59 = 125448750.
%e A399514 Some later values are:
%e A399514 a(100) = 2 * 3^7 * 5^23 * 7^4 * 11^5 * 89 = 1794462846588921546936035156250.
%e A399514 a(1000) = 2 * 3^4 * 5^230 * 7^10 * 11^22 * 89.
%e A399514 a(10^4) = 2 * 3 * 5^1091 * 7^16 * 11^953 * 89.
%e A399514 a(10^5) = 3^98 * 5^1788 * 7^4 * 11^15488 * 19.
%e A399514 a(10^6) = 3^95 * 5^213690 * 7^10 * 11^24225 * 19.
%t A399514 pigame = {365/46, 29/161, 79/575, 679/451, 3159/413, 83/407, 473/371, 638/355, 434/335, 89/235, 17/209, 79/122, 31/183, 41/115, 517/89, 111/83, 305/79, 23/73, 73/71, 61/67, 37/61, 19/59, 89/57, 41/53, 833/47, 53/43, 86/41, 13/38, 23/37, 67/31, 71/29, 83/19, 475/17, 59/13, 41/291, 1/7, 1/11, 1/1024, 1/97, 89};
%t A399514 NestList[SelectFirst[#*pigame, IntegerQ] &, 4, 50] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Sep 23 2026 *)
%o A399514 (PARI) A399514_upto(N,a=4)=vector(N+1,i,a=if(i>1,A395539(a),a))
%o A399514 (Python)
%o A399514 A399514=lambda upto=99:[(t:=A395539(t)if i else 4)for i in range(upto)]
%Y A399514 Cf. A395539 (definition of the PIGAME successor function), A399511 - A399513 (trajectory of 1, 2 and 3).
%K A399514 nonn,easy,new
%O A399514 0,1
%A A399514 _M. F. Hasler_, Sep 11 2026
